WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.610
- Hello everyone.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.670
I'm Maurice Samuels, the director

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.740
of the Yale Program for
the Study of Antisemitism.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.160
And it's my great pleasure to welcome you

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.160
to our first talk of the
2021-'22 academic year

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.830
in our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.970
I could not have asked
for a better speaker

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.190
to start things off.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.440
Jeffrey Herbst is the president

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.530
of the American Jewish University

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.830
in Los Angeles, California,
and has previously served

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.430
as the president of Colgate University,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.581
as president of the
Newseum in Washington, DC,

00:00:41.581 --> 00:00:44.800
and as provost of Miami University.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.820
He's a professor of political science

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.190
specializing in comparative
politics and is the author

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.720
of "State Politics in Zimbabwe," 1990,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.717
and "States and Power in Africa,

00:00:55.717 --> 00:01:00.060
"Comparative Lessons in
Authority and Control," of 2000,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.861
which received the Luebbert Best Book Award

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.560
in Comparative Politics

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.820
from the American Political
Science Association.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.760
I should add that Jeffrey
Herbst is a Yale alum.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.760
He received his PhD from
Yale in 1987, and when I read

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.640
the thoughtful and
thought provoking article

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.030
on campus antisemitism that
he published this summer

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.900
in Inside Higher Ed, and I
saw that he had gone to Yale,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.360
I realized that he would
be the perfect person

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.360
to help us think through the question

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.830
of how to talk about Middle
East politics on campus

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.620
in this time of escalating conflict.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.760
As some of you know, this
talk is all the more timely

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.120
after the recent discovery of racist

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.070
and antisemitic graffiti on
the Yale campus this past week.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.342
So here's how, excuse
me, things will work.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:53.860
President Herbst will speak
for about 30 or 40 minutes,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.810
after which he will take
questions from the audience.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.570
So please submit your questions
at any time during the talk,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.117
using the Q&amp;A button at
the bottom of your screen.

00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:07.730
And then I will ask those
questions anonymously.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.830
The chat function is disabled.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.830
So please don't use that,
and we will end at 6:00 PM.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.330
The title of today's talk is,

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.170
Antisemitism and Free
Speech on College Campuses.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.703
Please join me in
welcoming Jeffrey Herbst.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.030
- Professor Samuels,
thank you so very much.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.960
It's a pleasure to speak
to a Yale audience.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.900
I wish I could be in New
Haven, but I'm grateful

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.330
that we have the technology
that allows me to address you

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.003
on this important topic.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.275
To situate myself in this
discussion, I am a Jew,

00:02:44.275 --> 00:02:47.360
who's concerned, of
course, about antisemitism.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.310
And as Professor Samuels
said, I am president

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.420
of a faith-based institution and have been

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.400
a college administrator for some time.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.840
I'm also the former head of
the Newseum in Washington, DC,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.760
a cultural institution that was based

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.380
on the first amendment.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.790
So not only do I have an
appreciation of the value

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.690
of our free speech protections,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.150
but I have consistently
argued that the solution

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.690
to bad speech is more speech.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.340
I still believe that even
when there is an issue

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.370
such as antisemitism that I'm
personally concerned with.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.170
Of course, the free speech protections

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.490
of the first amendment
technically do not apply

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.690
to private colleges, such as Yale,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.500
because the free speech
guarantees in the constitution

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.260
are protections from
government censorship.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.850
However, I think we would all agree

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.400
that Yale and other
private colleges aspire

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.540
to the same free speech climate

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.700
that is constitutionally
guaranteed in public schools.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.710
And just to be clear, and to
make it even more complicated,

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.760
a significant amount
of what people consider

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.580
to be hate speech, including
some antisemitic remarks,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.420
are, contrary to public belief,
constitutionally protected.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.950
The ability to ban speech is constricted

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:01.010
and depends on the immediacy
and specificity of the threat.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.890
That being said, of course,
there's a great deal

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.910
of difference about what you can say

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.840
and what you should say.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.320
Finally, I approach this topic gingerly,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.770
especially as much of it
will focus on antisemitism

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.900
and the relationship to ongoing debate

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.960
about the Middle East because
charges and discussions

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:21.480
about antisemitism can
sometimes take all the oxygen

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.480
out of the room and cause
us to avoid other issues.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.050
For instance, in the
case of Nicholas Kristof,

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.097
the New York Times
correspondent who's argued,

00:04:32.097 --> 00:04:34.350
albeit in a tentative manner that Israel

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.060
should not defend itself
from Hamas rocket attacks,

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.690
Kristof defends himself by
saying, and it's not antisemitic

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.810
to criticize Israel for
possible war crimes.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.230
It's not, but it's also
could just be wrong.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.000
And the fact that critics
can sometimes be wrong,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.273
but not be antisemitic
is sometimes missed.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.310
Finally, in this discussion,
I think the stakes

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.610
are actually even higher than
for people, including Jews,

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.490
but not limited, of
course, who are worried

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.580
about antisemitism or people

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.020
who want to debate the Middle East.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.290
Residential colleges such as Yale,

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.330
along with military bases,
despite their many problems,

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.830
are among the most diverse
residential settings

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:20.060
in the United States, in
an increasingly polarized

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.210
and segmented society.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.720
Residential colleges
have a bedrock commitment

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.210
to making all feel part of their community

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.900
and to free speech, even if one or both

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.480
of those core commitments
are violated in practice.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.410
If there cannot be open,
passionate debate about Israel

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.640
and U.S. foreign policy in the Middle East

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.370
without lurching into antisemitism,

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.720
then I'm afraid there's not
much hope for good debate

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.670
in the rest of our society.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.590
If, on the other hand, as I hope,

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.877
and as I'll argue throughout this talk,

00:05:51.877 --> 00:05:54.930
colleges and universities can
model what civil discourse

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.030
actually means, then I think there is hope

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.200
for the rest of this society
also debating these issues

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.813
in a manner that's constructive.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.130
Of course, Israel has
been a contentious issue

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.650
on college campuses for decades.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.640
When I was a student at
Princeton in the early 1980s,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.500
Israel had invaded Lebanon,

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.880
and there was enormous
amount of controversy

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.960
about those actions.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.050
However, I was, at that
time, and for years after,

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.890
relatively sanguine about the
atmosphere for Jewish students

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.580
on most college campuses.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.150
Indeed, a decade ago, I was
giving talks where I argued

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.650
contrary to growing fears at the time,

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.300
that antisemitism on college campuses

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.310
was not that much of a problem

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:48.270
outside of a relatively few
large public universities.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.350
I wasn't wrong, I believe at the time,

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.120
but I think the situation has changed

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.620
and I'm concerned now, very concerned.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.220
The indicators regarding
college antisemitism

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.980
have been flashing red for some time.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.160
Hillel reported that antisemitic incidents

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.160
on college campuses rose to
an all time high in 2019-20,

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.500
even with the closure of
many due to the pandemic.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.822
There's been a constant drum
beat of antisemitic incidents

00:07:16.822 --> 00:07:19.050
that have grown disheartening.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.090
At the University of Southern California,

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.210
a Jewish student was forced
to resign as vice president

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.190
of the student government after harassment

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:29.830
because of her pro-Israel views.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.990
At Arizona State University,
Hitler Was Right posters

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:35.090
have been taken down several times.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.260
At the University of
Connecticut, there have been

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:40.430
repeated incidents of
antisemitic graffiti,

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.810
and verbal insults of the
type that Professor Samuels

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.240
reported on the Yale
campus just this week.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.500
A new poll surveying members of a leading

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:53.460
predominantly Jewish
fraternity and sorority found

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.360
that more than 65% of the respondents

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:00.550
have felt unsafe on campus
due to antisemitic attacks

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.670
with one in 10 reporting
that they have fear

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.210
of physical assault because
they are openly Jewish.

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.280
Additionally, nearly
70% of the students said

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.220
that personally experienced
or were familiar with an act

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.030
of antisemitism on campus or
in a virtual campus setting

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:19.350
in the last 120 days.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.610
As a result, and this is
particularly disheartening,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.220
about 50% said they had
hidden their Jewish identities

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.730
and more than half have avoided expressing

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.143
their views on Israel.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.880
That's terrible, both
for the students involved

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.870
and for all of us who
hope that a college campus

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:44.870
will be welcoming, safe and
openly vibrant for all students.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.130
More generally, across Jewish communities

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.450
in North America, there
is a real fear today

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:56.800
about the collegiate
environment that Jewish students

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.250
and supporters of Israel face.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.290
This has been fed by a
constant drip of stories

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:05.370
about physical confrontations
that Jewish students

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.419
have had as well as a feeling
that college administrators

00:09:08.419 --> 00:09:13.290
have not treated antisemitism
with the same seriousness

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.753
that they address other forms of hatred.

00:09:17.207 --> 00:09:19.450
It pains me to say this, but today,

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.280
I was talking to a friend
and I was telling her

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.930
that I was giving a
talk to a Yale audience,

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:30.930
and she told me that recently
her daughter had been advised

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:34.605
by a college counselor
not to apply to Yale

00:09:34.605 --> 00:09:39.605
because it had a difficult
environment for Jewish students.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.730
Now, I know it's very
difficult to place one story

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.330
in any particular context,
and I know there's a lot

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:50.740
of disinformation out
there and I see no data

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:53.740
that Yale is actually more
hostile to Jewish students

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.150
than any other campus or
certainly its competitors.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:01.420
However, that is the kind of
story and that is the kind

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:05.617
of discussion that is
out there and that is now

00:10:05.617 --> 00:10:09.570
being entertained by large
parts of the Jewish community

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:14.570
that would, I think, have been
inconceivable a decade ago.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.653
So I think the concern
should be very real.

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.280
Now, why is all this happening?

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.840
Part of it that a situation
in the United States

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.790
is becoming more polarized,
public expressions of hatred

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:31.930
towards many different
groups are increasing,

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:35.130
and this inevitably falls
partially to the Jews.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.370
According to recent FBI statistics,

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.200
American Jews, who make up no more than 2%

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:44.100
of the U.S. population
are targets of nearly 60%

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.460
of religious bias.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:50.510
Antisemitism on college
campuses has, in particular,

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:55.180
morphed over time from
old stereotypes about Jews

00:10:55.180 --> 00:11:00.180
to collateral damage from
heated discussions about Israel,

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.820
Palestine, the Middle East
and the United States' role

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.670
in that region.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.930
This is somewhat different than debates

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.460
in the public outside of colleges,

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:14.670
where old stereotypes of
Jews still often dominate

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:21.070
antisemitic attacks, and
certainly, we saw in the upsurge

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:27.500
in violence against Jews
across U.S. cities in May,

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.380
as a result of the Gaza conflict,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:35.370
that there is a continuing
rise of antisemitism,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:40.370
and this plays back on college
campuses where the dynamic

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:42.830
about heated debates about the Middle East

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:47.330
are turning into, sometimes,
antisemitic attacks

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:51.083
has been of primary
concern to Jewish students.

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:56.490
What else is different, I think,

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:59.336
is that the institutional
settings of debate

00:11:59.336 --> 00:12:02.260
about Israel, Palestine, the Middle East,

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.550
the United States' role
is being challenged.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:07.900
And I think this is of particular concern

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.270
to people as myself, who
both value a safe environment

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:15.053
for all students and one
which promotes free speech.

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:22.610
Opponents of Israel
have taken as a strategy

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:25.210
from migrating from individual voice,

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:28.140
which I view as protected
and often welcome,

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.520
to demanding that institutions
on college campuses

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.580
take positions, something
that I view as frightening

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:36.670
and quite often inappropriate.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:39.880
Here I'll focus in part
on the Yale campus,

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.470
both because I'm nominally
speaking to a Yale audience,

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.060
and because as Professor Samuels noted,

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.740
I'm an alum of this institution.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.540
But also, because what's
playing out on the Yale campus

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.200
is playing out on many
other college campuses

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.330
across this country.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:59.010
Now, of course, many scholars, no doubt,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.570
including some at Yale,
have signed letters

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:05.740
in their personal capacity
strongly opposing Israel

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:07.540
during the Gaza violence.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.190
I can argue with these individuals who,

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.743
I believe, are sometimes
right, sometimes wrong,

00:13:12.743 --> 00:13:16.520
sometimes misinformed,
sometimes antisemitic.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.210
However, they're using
their voice to express

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:23.720
their individual opinion and
I or others in the community

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:27.590
are free to disagree with them
on an equal playing field.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:30.400
While I might regret
personally their views,

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.230
I defend their right in
most cases to have them,

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.350
and I look forward to debating them.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:39.493
However, what has changed is that now,

00:13:39.493 --> 00:13:42.360
some institutions on college campuses

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.880
are being asked to take positions.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.610
As was noted widely on
your campus in June,

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:51.200
the Yale College Council
adopted a statement

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:54.237
of condemnation of Israel
that noted, in part,

00:13:54.237 --> 00:13:56.697
"As Yale students, we
condemn the injustice,

00:13:56.697 --> 00:14:00.650
"ethnic cleansing and genocide
occurring in Palestine,"

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:03.767
and continued, "Just as
Israel's military imposes

00:14:03.767 --> 00:14:06.437
"the apartheid system
against Palestinians,

00:14:06.437 --> 00:14:09.597
"the U.S. police enforces
a system of white supremacy

00:14:09.597 --> 00:14:11.357
"against Black Americans."

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.050
Now I view those statements
as exceptionally problematic,

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:18.050
because I do not believe,
and I can argue this

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:21.760
at greater length, that
ethnic cleansing or genocide,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:25.300
in fact, characterizes
what is going on in Israel

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:28.200
and the Palestinian Territories.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.430
And as someone who observed
firsthand, who studied,

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.720
wrote about apartheid in South Africa,

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:37.350
I do not believe that it is an application

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:40.880
where Arab-Israelis have full
political rights in Israel,

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.480
and where there is now
an Arab political party

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.063
in the ruling coalition.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.062
I don't know the exact
origins of their resolution,

00:14:50.062 --> 00:14:52.640
and I would surmise that
there was a fair amount

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.030
of virtue signaling and other politicking.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.600
I do know that it was unfortunate,

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.830
because I think that a student
government could have done

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:06.830
a good turn in setting
parameters for an open, free,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.510
fierce debate about Israel, but instead,

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:13.750
went in another direction, and
in fact, closing off debate

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.510
and adopting a position
which it knew many students

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.313
would disagree with or
feel comfortable with.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:25.140
Still, these are young people,
and as Deborah Lipstadt,

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:28.460
the Biden administration's
nominee for a special envoy

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.250
to fight antisemitism has noted,

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:34.010
when looking at antisemites,
we should differentiate

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.960
between the simple one and the wicked one.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:40.150
As regrettable as the student
government's views were,

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:42.570
I'm under no illusion
that students governments

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.220
are particularly important
and have much of a power

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:47.740
to structure conversation on campus.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.630
It is certainly possible, of
course, that the statement

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:53.310
could feel threatening
to individual students,

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:58.240
Jewish or non-Jewish, who
are supportive of Israel,

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.070
and that would be unfortunate.

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:03.680
More likely, it will lead
to the de-legitimization

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.563
of the student government itself.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.410
Of course, there's not much
the Yale administration

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.630
could have done about what the
student government proclaims.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:14.830
It could have issued a statement contrary,

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.840
but I understand why
administrators might be reluctant

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.313
to react to every student
government statement.

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:24.500
Much more problematic in my view

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.300
were some faculty
statements that have emerged

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:28.883
from particular programs.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:32.690
The faculty in Ethnicity,
Race and Migration,

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:35.897
issued a statement on Palestine
on May 17th that said,

00:16:35.897 --> 00:16:37.427
"As scholars in the program,

00:16:37.427 --> 00:16:39.737
"we recognize the Palestinian struggle

00:16:39.737 --> 00:16:42.327
"as an indigenous liberation movement

00:16:42.327 --> 00:16:43.847
"confronting a settler colonial state

00:16:43.847 --> 00:16:48.020
"illegally expropriating
their land for over 70 years."

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:51.837
Members of the Yale
program went on to say,

00:16:51.837 --> 00:16:54.367
"We express solidarity
with the Palestinian people

00:16:54.367 --> 00:16:57.607
"as they resist the many forms
of Israeli state violence

00:16:57.607 --> 00:16:59.427
"and fight for their freedom.

00:16:59.427 --> 00:17:02.607
"We will amplify the voices
of Palestinian scholars

00:17:02.607 --> 00:17:05.077
"and center the anti-colonial struggles

00:17:05.077 --> 00:17:08.290
"in our academic and political practice."

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:12.680
No talk here, of course,
to return to the 1967 lines

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:13.890
or anything else.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.920
This is about the
de-legitimization of Israel

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.550
and the promotion of a
particular perspective,

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:23.470
and as they say, their
academic and public practice.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.470
Similarly, the Yale's Women,
Gender and Sexuality Studies

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:29.870
program signed a letter,
also endorsed by dozens

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.630
of other women's and
gender studies programs

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.550
across the country, titled,

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:36.860
Gender Studies Departments in Solidary

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:39.500
with Palestinian Feminist Collective,

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.240
that, among other things,
endorsed the unambiguous right

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.150
of return for Palestinians,
which depending

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.470
on what this actually means would amount

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.870
to the destruction of
Israel as a Jewish state.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:52.457
Further, the letter said,

00:17:52.457 --> 00:17:54.857
"We do not subscribe to a," quote,

00:17:54.857 --> 00:17:58.037
"both sides," unquote,
"rhetoric that erases

00:17:58.037 --> 00:18:01.177
"the military, economic,
media and global power

00:18:01.177 --> 00:18:03.507
"that Israel has over Palestine.

00:18:03.507 --> 00:18:05.817
"This is not a conflict," in quote,

00:18:05.817 --> 00:18:08.100
"that is too controversial and complex,"

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.127
in quotes, "to assess."

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.790
I'll get to the substantive
issues with regard to Israel

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:19.820
in a moment, but I think
stepping back from Israel

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.810
and antisemitism that these statements

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:26.153
are exceptionally problematic
for a number of reasons.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:30.005
Speech and debate, the very
things that these programs

00:18:30.005 --> 00:18:33.980
and others at a university
were designed to encourage

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:35.590
are being stifled.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:38.390
Here, I'm concerned about
the boundaries of free speech

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:41.280
being curtailed because
those who have the power

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:44.010
and make no mistake, the
professors and faculty

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.600
in these programs speak
from a privileged position

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.300
in the hierarchy at Yale,

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:50.500
are predetermining in the outcome

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:52.683
of certain intellectual debates.

00:18:52.683 --> 00:18:55.800
Indeed, they are de-legitimizing anyone

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:57.393
who has an opposing view.

00:18:58.768 --> 00:19:00.960
No less than the American Association

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.960
of University Professors, in
its 1940 profound statement

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.170
on principles on academic
freedom and tenure,

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.530
defined the prevailing
professional standards

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:13.890
of academic freedom of faculty.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:17.067
It stated, "As scholars
and educational officers,

00:19:17.067 --> 00:19:19.017
"they, teachers, should
remember that the public

00:19:19.017 --> 00:19:21.547
"may judge their profession
and their institution

00:19:21.547 --> 00:19:23.027
"by their utterances.

00:19:23.027 --> 00:19:25.137
"Hence they should, at
all times be accurate,

00:19:25.137 --> 00:19:27.487
"should exercise appropriate restraint,

00:19:27.487 --> 00:19:31.607
"should show respect for
the opinion of others

00:19:31.607 --> 00:19:33.527
"and should make every effort to indicate

00:19:33.527 --> 00:19:37.130
"that they are not speaking
for the institution."

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:41.090
Academic freedom is a
wonderful thing, but it comes

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.070
with certain profound responsibilities.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:49.040
The implications of programs
taking on political positions

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.610
is that certain types
of research and outcomes

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:54.220
are privileged as well as implications

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:55.513
for hiring and teaching.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:00.960
At its basis, I think this is
profoundly anti-intellectual,

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.580
by not only arguing the
other side is wrong,

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:06.840
but stating, really,
there is no other side.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.230
Now I would say the exact same thing

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:12.640
if an East Asian Studies
department issued proclamations

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.860
about genocide against the Uyghurs.

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:16.950
Even though I personally
believe that genocide

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:20.189
is being conducted by
Beijing, but I don't believe

00:20:20.189 --> 00:20:23.550
it's the role of an academic department

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:28.530
to take on political positions
that change the contours

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:31.250
of the free speech debate on campus.

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:33.770
I headed the Department
of Politics at Princeton

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.397
for five years, which I
believe is a distinguished

00:20:36.397 --> 00:20:39.620
academic unit, and I
also have a lot of firm

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:42.090
and deeply held political beliefs.

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:44.240
It would never occur to me, frankly,

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:47.540
to get to the department to
endorse the positions I had

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.640
both because I didn't believe that others

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.240
necessarily believed them,
and I would've been worried

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:56.150
on the implications for our
students, graduate student,

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:59.140
departmental research and
the intellectual climate

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:01.463
that the department
was supposed to foster.

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:07.130
Jewish students and
those who are pro-Israel,

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:09.350
could legitimately complain, I think,

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.240
that these proclamations and others

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.490
cause a threatening environment.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:16.390
I have no idea about the
individual sentiments

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:19.830
of the faculty who have
signed these proclamations,

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.830
but I know that these kinds of statements,

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:25.800
by de-legitimizing certain
intellectual positions,

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:30.082
provide an environment for
attack on supporters of Israel

00:21:30.082 --> 00:21:33.140
and inevitably, because
their positions are seen

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:37.000
as illegitimate, attacks
which can verge on,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.420
or leap over to antisemitism.

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:43.840
And of course, values that
support the resistance

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.120
without differentiation
of the Palestinians

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:50.070
is not even a veiled endorsement of Hamas.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.890
It is an endorsement of
Hamas and its decision

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:55.040
to rain rockets down on Israelis,

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:57.220
both Jews and Palestinians.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:00.040
It would have been easier to differentiate

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:04.700
some sort of support, vis-a-vis
some sorts of resistance,

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:08.260
but that was not done,
leading these programs

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:13.260
to implicitly endorse the
proudly antisemitic organization,

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:17.413
Hamas, that has at its
fundamental charter,

00:22:17.413 --> 00:22:21.080
calling for the destruction
of the Jewish state.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:22.500
Now it's my understanding,

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.300
although I'm not well aware
of the campus environment,

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:28.140
that many at Yale have not spoken out

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:30.183
against these programs' statements,

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.280
despite the fact that I believe

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:35.330
that they are significant departures

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:37.260
from academic standards.

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:39.840
I view that as unfortunate.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.380
As someone who believes
that ethnicity, race,

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:46.370
migration and gender are
critically important to understand,

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:50.050
and who has tried to contribute
in some of those areas

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:53.920
and as a college administrator,
I would say, then,

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.910
when you do not engage with
colleagues who you believe

00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:00.910
have violated the norms of the university,

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:03.300
you are essentially marginalizing them,

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:07.500
essentially not taking them
seriously enough to disagree.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:10.147
As another Yalie has said,

00:23:10.147 --> 00:23:12.777
"Those who do not confront these programs

00:23:12.777 --> 00:23:16.007
"are guilty of the soft
bigotry of low expectations."

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:21.760
Why have universities
had such trouble dealing

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:25.770
with antisemitism and
why is there such concern

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:29.400
among Jewish students
and significant worry

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.540
that the problem will get
worse in the coming years,

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:35.170
despite the fact that many universities

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:37.890
with the best of
intentions, including Yale,

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:42.240
have devoted enormous resources
to student affairs offices,

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:47.003
to diversity, to bias
training and the like?

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:51.670
Part of the answer is
that fighting hatred,

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:54.650
fighting bigotry on campuses is hard,

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:57.460
and I recognize that full stop.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:23:59.280
But there are also other issues

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:02.273
that are specific to antisemitism.

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:07.370
Jewish students are often coded as white

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:11.320
by student affairs officers and by others.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:15.200
Seen as a privileged portion of society,

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:17.960
the fact that these
students may be vulnerable

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:21.780
to antisemitic attacks is
not put on the same plane

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:25.050
of concern as possibilities of bigotry

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:28.790
and hatred devoted against
communities of color

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:31.620
who have, of course,
endured a tremendous amount

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.580
of bigotry and hatred.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:36.820
This is despite the fact
that the recently released

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:40.330
Pew study on American Jewry found that 15%

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:44.294
of Jews under the age of
30, might reasonably include

00:24:44.294 --> 00:24:48.387
the college population,
identify as Hispanic, Black,

00:24:48.387 --> 00:24:51.000
Asian, or multi-racial.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.610
Of course, no students
should be subject to hatred

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:58.130
or bigotry, but the fact
that Jews are not coded

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:01.570
as among those groups who
are traditionally been

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.470
the recipient of tremendous
hatred and bigotry,

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:07.490
produces, I would argue, some dissonance

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:12.130
in collegiate approaches to
how to deal with the problem.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:17.100
It's also the case that some
portion, but by no means all,

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:20.873
of the antisemitic
attacks on Jewish students

00:25:20.873 --> 00:25:24.530
who are supporters of
Israel come from people

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:27.540
who are involved in communities of color

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.300
and who college administrators code

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:34.080
as always being the recipients
of hatred and bigotry,

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:36.943
and not necessarily as the perpetrators.

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.200
What to do.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:44.430
The common answer is always more speech.

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:49.040
That's been my answer for a long time,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:53.050
but isn't what I'm saying is
that there's too much focus

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:55.850
on Israel right now, and the sheer focus

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:59.220
is part of what's propelling antisemitism?

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:02.500
I don't believe that, and
I'm fine with a lot of focus

00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:05.240
on Israel, but I think it has to be done

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:08.000
with nuance and with care.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.790
Although it's obvious, it is worth saying

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:15.250
that not every criticism
of Israel is antisemitic.

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:17.960
There are plenty of legitimate criticisms

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:22.030
and although heroic, powerful
voices like Natan Sharansky,

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:25.130
have argued that it's
antisemitic if Israel is treated

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:27.330
in a fundamentally different manner,

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:29.210
I'm not even sure of that.

00:26:29.210 --> 00:26:31.570
On some level, it's true, of course,

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:34.558
but I don't take it that Israel,

00:26:34.558 --> 00:26:37.630
but I take it that Israel should be held

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:41.080
to a higher standard than
Syria or other countries

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:45.010
in the Middle East because of
its own self-professed norms.

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:47.500
It wants to be held to a higher standards.

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:51.670
And yes, because it is a
major U.S. aid recipient.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:55.360
However, that makes it
special, but not unique,

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:57.733
a differentiation that is important.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:01.930
And I'm not sure why there
isn't additional speech

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:02.813
on other issues.

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:07.130
Take for instance, Ethiopia,
a country that I've studied,

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:09.787
that's part of my own academic work.

00:27:09.787 --> 00:27:13.350
Ethiopia is a country that
the U.S. has given $13 billion

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:17.580
in aid over the last 20
years and that received,

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:19.830
until the recent civil war began,

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:22.690
around $3.5 billion in foreign assistance

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:25.750
from major donors each
year, including another

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:28.670
significant share from
multilateral institutions

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:31.600
which Americans, in part, share.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:34.320
Because of the civil war
instigated in good part

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:36.370
by the federal government in Addis,

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:39.490
over 400,000 people,
according to a recent report

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:41.430
by the Council on Foreign Relations,

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.750
in Tigray Province are
living in famine conditions.

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:47.700
79% of pregnant and lactating women

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:49.560
screened over the course of a week

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:52.100
were found to suffer
from acute malnutrition

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:55.100
and only 11% of the
humanitarian supplies needed

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:58.800
in the region have actually
been delivered since July.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.210
Delivery of medical supplies
to Tigray is prohibited

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:06.000
by the Ethiopian government,
as is the delivery of fuel,

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:10.100
making distribution of aid
that does make it to the region

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:12.070
extraordinarily difficult.

00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:14.040
Now I'm not saying of course,

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:15.760
that you cannot talk about Israel

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.200
if you don't talk about Ethiopia.

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:20.620
What I am saying is
there should be more talk

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:23.230
about Ethiopia and in particular,

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:26.320
understanding that the world
is a complicated place.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:29.850
If you come to an understanding
and a critique of Israel

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:33.770
after at least some review
of what is happening

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:36.070
in the rest of the world, then you have

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.250
a potentially respectable position

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.560
or at least one that can be argued with.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:44.600
However, if you immediately
look at Israel is uniquely bad

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:47.590
without understanding how the world works,

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:49.310
then that is something else.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.450
The Yale College Council has not taken

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:53.800
a thundering declaration on Tigray,

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.270
and women's studies programs

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.743
do not talk about women in Tigray.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.100
Even closer to home,

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:03.423
I often wonder about the focus on Israel.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.450
Here, I'll talk briefly about the Biden

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:08.980
and Obama administrations.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:11.422
I'll skip Trump for the moment
only because the two others

00:29:11.422 --> 00:29:13.503
are the ones likely that are supported

00:29:13.503 --> 00:29:15.950
by a good part of the audience.

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:19.850
President Biden's administration
killed Zamari Ahmadi,

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:23.650
it's important to say his name,
by a drone in Afghanistan,

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:27.050
after he was going around
Kabul collecting water bottles.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.660
Seven children were killed
in collateral damage

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.930
because of that drone attack.

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:36.000
Yet there has not been a
demand for a war crimes trial

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:38.880
for a military that
killed a person of color.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:42.370
I can only imagine if
Netanyahu had done this.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:44.200
More generally, according to the Bureau

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:49.040
of Investigative Journalism,
a total of 563 strikes,

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:53.100
largely by drones, targeted
Pakistan, Somalia and Yemen

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:55.450
during Obama's two terms,

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:58.070
about 10 times as many as under Bush.

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:00.990
Between 384 and 800 civilians were killed

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:04.790
in those countries, according
to a report log by the bureau.

00:30:04.790 --> 00:30:09.060
And of course those countries,
unlike Gaza and Israel,

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:12.830
did not provide any direct
threat to the United States,

00:30:12.830 --> 00:30:14.980
and I've been struck by
the fact that there's often

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:19.630
more debate about the
Israeli conduct of conflict

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:21.940
in the United States than there has been

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.290
about the American conduct of conflict.

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:26.870
Although you would think that Americans

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:29.120
are most concerned about the governments

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:31.563
that they themselves elect directly.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.230
Now, why is this?

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:36.020
Well, lots of people are passionate

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:37.600
about Israel and Palestine.

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:39.010
I get that.

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:42.260
It's easier to cover
Gaza than it is Yemen.

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:43.830
That's an issue.

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:46.070
Some Americans are more
interested in criticizing

00:30:46.070 --> 00:30:48.130
Netanyahu, who was still prime minister

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:50.180
during then the last Gaza conflict

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:53.050
than in the own leaders that they support.

00:30:53.050 --> 00:30:55.560
That's less okay, I think.

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:58.480
Hamas has made a a tactical decision

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:01.480
to make its people vulnerable
and then use their deaths

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:05.490
as de-legitimating the
Israeli right to defense.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:08.917
It has its supporters
and its fellow travelers

00:31:08.917 --> 00:31:12.890
who have fallen in with
its media strategy.

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:17.890
And then, the Israel
conflict with Palestinians

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:21.760
has been used to link to U.S. conflict.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:23.920
From Minneapolis to Palestine,

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:27.070
as the documents the Yale
students published say.

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:29.750
I don't wanna spend too
much time on this issue,

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:34.120
but I'll say, as someone
who's done field work

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.410
in countries with conflict,

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:42.100
that participants on the ground
understand their conflict

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:45.140
according to their own circumstances

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:48.830
without reference to
Minnesota or anywhere else.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:50.760
You demean local participants,

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:52.790
both Israelis and Palestinians,

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:55.530
when you try to understand
them by placing them

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:57.710
in your conflict.

00:31:57.710 --> 00:32:01.860
So, and then there are, of
course, outright antisemites

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:06.040
who jump all over any
controversy about Israel

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:08.413
to feed in their own bigotry.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:12.410
What is clear is there's a
whole range of mixed motives

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:15.860
when there's more speech about Israel.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:19.780
Some of it, a large part of
it, I would argue, is good.

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:22.470
Some of it is problematic.

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:25.670
What I do believe, what
I am in favor of is

00:32:25.670 --> 00:32:29.400
when people such as
myself say more speech,

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.520
we really should say more careful speech.

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:35.680
I'm not interested in censoring anyone,

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:39.470
but I am interested in
encouraging everyone

00:32:39.470 --> 00:32:42.420
to spend the time to
understand the nuances

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:46.320
and complexities of yes,
all sides of a conflict

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:48.740
and to try to put them into context

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.993
of the way the world operates.

00:32:52.960 --> 00:32:57.010
The gain, the political
discussion has to be

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:58.630
on an even keel.

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.600
That's why I'm somewhat concerned

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.190
about student governments who can poison

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:06.560
collegiate atmospheres,
but I'm very concerned

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:11.160
about academic programs in
institutions that should serve

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:14.530
as a venue for arguments, not as a vector

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:17.910
for particular opinions
and who develop, in fact,

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:21.040
consciously anti-intellectual stances.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:24.770
These programs and departments
go against the very idea

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:28.423
of the more careful speech
which I'm trying to promote.

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:33.220
To conclude, I'll say that
antisemitism is a problem

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:37.100
that suggests that, all data
suggests is going to get worse

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:40.810
on society at large and
on college campuses.

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:45.140
It is going to be an ongoing
concern and not go away.

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:48.650
And we are on the verge, as I
wrote in my article in August,

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:51.250
of some potentially very serious incidents

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:54.460
where physical harm is done to students,

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:55.970
and there's already a great deal

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:58.700
of mental and emotional anguish.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:01.720
Colleges and universities
should go out of their way

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:05.340
to be proven grounds to
show how difficult issues

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.720
which people feel passionately
about can be discussed

00:34:08.720 --> 00:34:10.870
without bigotry and animus.

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:13.630
Instead, they're going
in the opposite direction

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:15.437
and maybe the places where Israel

00:34:15.437 --> 00:34:17.510
and the Middle East are least likely

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:19.840
to be productively discussed.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:21.970
I think that would be unfortunate.

00:34:21.970 --> 00:34:25.140
I think college administrators
should go out of their way

00:34:25.140 --> 00:34:27.737
to socialize their
colleagues that antisemitism

00:34:27.737 --> 00:34:31.780
is a real issue, that bigotry can affect

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:35.750
even those populations who
may be coded as privileged

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:38.950
and that administrators
are going to have to do

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:41.610
a tremendous amount of work and exert

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.740
significant political capital to make sure

00:34:44.740 --> 00:34:47.780
that the speech environment
on their campuses

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:50.600
remains free and fair.

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:54.620
If we can do that, if we can
figure out a way to discuss

00:34:54.620 --> 00:34:59.170
these issues without bigotry,
then I believe that colleges

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:02.260
and universities can
reassert their stature

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:06.580
as the primary intellectual
centers of American life

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:10.980
and model to a society,
which is polarized, divided,

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:13.850
and which is rife with bigotry and hatred,

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:16.290
how discussions can happen.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:19.520
I'm not sure that will
occur on all campuses,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:22.680
but across the 4,000
colleges and universities

00:35:22.680 --> 00:35:26.130
in the United States, I
hope that these issues

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:29.340
so vitally important to
Israelis, Palestinians,

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:32.530
Jews, non-Jews, and a
great many other people

00:35:32.530 --> 00:35:35.463
across the world can be developed in a way

00:35:35.463 --> 00:35:39.430
that people feel is productive, important

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:41.980
and free of fear and hostility.

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.360
Professor Samuels, let me stop there.

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:47.380
As I said, I'm honored to
be able to talk with you,

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:49.960
delighted to be able to
address these subjects,

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:53.550
happy to answer any questions
that the audience may have.

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:54.410
- Okay. Terrific.

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:55.981
Thank you, Jeffrey.

00:35:55.981 --> 00:35:58.721
We're really very grateful
for that really lucid

00:35:58.721 --> 00:36:02.180
and nuanced presentation,
and I'm sure there gonna be

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:03.630
a lot of questions.

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:05.360
There already are some.

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:07.340
I ask, just to remind everybody,

00:36:07.340 --> 00:36:11.080
to please submit your questions
using the Q&amp;A function,

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.652
and I will ask them anonymously.

00:36:13.652 --> 00:36:17.600
So while those are coming in,
I did have a few questions

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:20.570
of my own to get us going.

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:23.720
First of all, I was wondering
if you could just go back

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:28.680
the way you started the
presentation with the question

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:31.490
of freedom of speech, because
I think that that is something

00:36:31.490 --> 00:36:34.916
that people find confusing and especially,

00:36:34.916 --> 00:36:39.030
the distinction between public
and private universities,

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:42.500
is there any difference
in what kind of speech

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:45.512
is protected and not?

00:36:45.512 --> 00:36:48.267
- The first amendment begins with

00:36:48.267 --> 00:36:50.430
"Congress shall make no law."

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:53.080
So it applies to government censorship,

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:55.930
and it's been understood that it applies

00:36:55.930 --> 00:37:00.228
to public universities that
are part of the American state.

00:37:00.228 --> 00:37:03.720
So formally, the first amendment applies

00:37:03.720 --> 00:37:08.720
to public universities, but
I'm sure my colleagues at Yale

00:37:09.940 --> 00:37:12.470
and every place else,
certainly I felt this way

00:37:12.470 --> 00:37:16.380
at Colgate and Princeton,
believe that everyone

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:19.302
on those campuses should have the same

00:37:19.302 --> 00:37:23.270
rights of expression that
are formally guaranteed

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:27.558
when I was at Miami University
in Ohio, a public university.

00:37:27.558 --> 00:37:30.547
So I think the constitution
may be one thing,

00:37:30.547 --> 00:37:35.410
but I think the norms
that have been adopted

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.960
certainly suggest that
private universities

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:41.803
have aspired to the same
free speech environments

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:45.993
that public universities
are required to have.

00:37:46.910 --> 00:37:48.530
- Okay. Thank you for that.

00:37:48.530 --> 00:37:52.130
So here's a question that
I had, but I hear it echoed

00:37:52.130 --> 00:37:54.713
in some of the questions
from the audience.

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:59.040
Can you be a little more specific

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:04.040
about why those statements
by the Yale College Council

00:38:04.330 --> 00:38:07.150
or by the Ethnicity, Race and Migration,

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:10.250
I, we understand why you
think it's problematic,

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:13.900
but can you be a little
more specific about whether

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:16.870
and why you think that
they're antisemitic?

00:38:16.870 --> 00:38:19.380
So, in other words, where is that,

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:21.340
this is another way of
asking the question,

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:25.780
where is the line between
anti-Zionism and antisemitism?

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:30.128
- I hope I was nuanced,
enough in my presentation.

00:38:30.128 --> 00:38:33.490
I said that they can enable an environment

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:36.980
where attacks on supporters
of Israel can verge on

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:39.243
or lurch over to antisemitism.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:45.980
That's the important issue,
to me, because first of all,

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:50.020
those statements change
the level playing field

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:53.050
that individuals have in
debating these issues.

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.921
And second, they go out of their way,

00:38:55.921 --> 00:38:58.210
they were quite striking, I thought,

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:01.670
in de-legitimizing the other side.

00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:05.850
It's not they said, we think
that the other side is wrong

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:09.940
on the facts or on the
data, or on our opinion.

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:14.245
They said it's illegitimate
to have a different opinion.

00:39:14.245 --> 00:39:17.520
And the first statement
also said some things

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:20.270
about privileging certain perspectives

00:39:20.270 --> 00:39:22.206
in the academic practice.

00:39:22.206 --> 00:39:27.206
That has, I'm not saying that
that per se is antisemitic.

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:32.860
What I am saying is that enables
an antisemitic environment,

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:37.720
and I think is, before we
even get to antisemitism,

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:39.490
just exceptionally problematic

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:41.370
in terms of academic practice.

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:42.975
- Mm-hmm. Okay.

00:39:42.975 --> 00:39:47.720
So another question that,
you know, as someone,

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:51.940
I think we both went
to college in the '80s,

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:56.170
when, and you referred to
the anti-apartheid movement

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:58.120
in the '80s, and I think a lot of people,

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:00.808
especially from our
generation, but others,

00:40:00.808 --> 00:40:03.210
have a hard time understanding
what the difference is

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:07.840
between, you know, opposing
apartheid in the '80s

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:11.780
and opposing either just Israel to cour,

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:16.400
or the occupation, let's say now.

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:18.670
- Look, I mean, if you wanna
take a position on Israel

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:20.640
and oppose the occupation
as an individual,

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:22.170
that's your basis.

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:27.010
What I'm saying is that I was not aware,

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:29.540
when I was at Princeton or Yale,

00:40:29.540 --> 00:40:31.010
when I was a graduate student

00:40:31.010 --> 00:40:35.817
and Yale was a center of
Southern African studies

00:40:35.817 --> 00:40:38.010
in the 1980s at the heart of it,

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:40.120
at the height of the apartheid conflict,

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:42.340
that individual programs were taking

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:45.244
political positions on this.

00:40:45.244 --> 00:40:49.042
Yale's response to the divestment debate

00:40:49.042 --> 00:40:51.950
in the 1980s was a wise one.

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:53.850
It said, we're not gonna divest,

00:40:53.850 --> 00:40:56.800
but we're going to invest
significant resources

00:40:56.800 --> 00:40:58.870
in the study of Southern Africa,

00:40:58.870 --> 00:41:01.197
and develop what I believe
was the premiere center

00:41:01.197 --> 00:41:03.490
for Southern African Studies at the time.

00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:05.185
I thought that was exactly right.

00:41:05.185 --> 00:41:10.185
When you have programs and
institutions take an opinion,

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:15.580
I think that changes
the nature of discourse.

00:41:15.580 --> 00:41:17.650
And I think it can be read as part

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:22.450
of the politicization of
everything that we see.

00:41:22.450 --> 00:41:24.370
Fewer personal relationships,

00:41:24.370 --> 00:41:27.066
friendships across party lines,

00:41:27.066 --> 00:41:32.066
fewer people trusting any
source from the other side,

00:41:33.479 --> 00:41:36.970
and this politicization
of everything also fueled

00:41:36.970 --> 00:41:39.170
by social media in its own way,

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:44.170
has leeched onto college
campuses, and it's a problem.

00:41:44.411 --> 00:41:49.411
And I think it's one,
irrespective, I think,

00:41:49.514 --> 00:41:53.210
as I said, of your opinion
on Israel, that I would hope

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:54.970
people would find problematic.

00:41:54.970 --> 00:41:58.170
Just like I said, if a department
started taking positions

00:41:58.170 --> 00:42:01.460
on other issues, even issues I agree with,

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:03.350
I would find it problematic.

00:42:03.350 --> 00:42:06.740
- So I think to play devil's advocate,

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:11.030
and I have definitely heard this argument

00:42:12.580 --> 00:42:15.210
from people involved, especially, I think,

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:20.210
in Women, Gender and Sexuality Studies,

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:24.240
Ethnicity, Race and
Migration, they would say,

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:29.240
or I have heard some people
say that their scholarship

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:34.530
is about that kind of
direct political action.

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:38.270
So they don't really acknowledge, I think,

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:41.790
a distinction between their scholarship

00:42:44.680 --> 00:42:46.350
and their advocacy, let's say.

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:47.720
So how do you-

00:42:47.720 --> 00:42:51.406
- I would say their
scholarship is their business

00:42:51.406 --> 00:42:55.080
and their individual
advocacy is their business,

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:59.070
and they have the right to
pursue those intellectual

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:02.030
and advocacy paths, absolutely.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:05.930
It's where you invoke
institutional authority

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:07.730
that I think the line is crossed.

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:08.700
- Mm-hmm.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:11.810
Okay, and what, there's
an interesting question

00:43:11.810 --> 00:43:16.810
from the audience about,
there's been so much in the news

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:22.050
just this week about the
influence of social media

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:23.600
on these questions.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:28.600
So can you say something about
how social media platforms

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:31.890
have kind of driven the
kind of polarization

00:43:31.890 --> 00:43:33.690
that we're seeing on college campuses?

00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:34.523
- Yeah.

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:39.720
This is a generalization,
but I'll start here.

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:42.980
In the 1980s, when the College Democrats

00:43:42.980 --> 00:43:46.270
and the College Republicans
on a particular campus,

00:43:46.270 --> 00:43:50.010
had to find someone to
talk with and debate,

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:52.710
they found each other and
they debated each other

00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:55.200
and maybe they agreed,
or maybe they disagreed,

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:57.150
but they were talking with each other

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:59.120
and being human beings,

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:01.270
they recognized each other's humanity

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:05.500
and friendships and social ties developed.

00:44:05.500 --> 00:44:08.500
Now people can live if
they're College Democrats

00:44:08.500 --> 00:44:10.620
or College Republicans or anything else,

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:13.720
in their own ecosystem on social media,

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:18.080
without finding anyone who
ever disagrees with them

00:44:18.080 --> 00:44:21.050
and any reason to moderate your views.

00:44:21.050 --> 00:44:23.810
So the filter bubble of social media,

00:44:23.810 --> 00:44:26.850
to borrow from a well-known praise,

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:29.990
I think has caused the
increasing polarization

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:34.170
of American society on
just about every issue,

00:44:34.170 --> 00:44:37.961
because the need to mediate positions

00:44:37.961 --> 00:44:40.580
in order to be culturally acceptable,

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:43.410
in order to develop
ties is no longer there.

00:44:43.410 --> 00:44:48.160
And in fact, almost anyone,
with no matter how extreme views

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:52.400
can find others who share
those views, when in the past,

00:44:52.400 --> 00:44:54.080
there might've been others out there,

00:44:54.080 --> 00:44:56.540
but they weren't in the same town.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:45:00.490
So social media, which I
have a, at the Newseum,

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:03.630
I celebrated social media
as the greatest explosion

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:06.470
of public expression in human history.

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:08.286
I still believe that

00:45:08.286 --> 00:45:11.670
and many wonderful things have occurred.

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:14.480
As another saying goes,
the people formerly known

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:18.110
as the audience now are able to speak,

00:45:18.110 --> 00:45:22.220
but some of what the
audience says, we found out,

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:27.220
some of what we've been thinking
privately, in some cases,

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.330
turns out to be problematic.

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:32.210
And along with a great
increase in democratization

00:45:32.210 --> 00:45:35.370
has come increased expressions
of bigotry, hatred,

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:38.810
across many different levels,
expressions of violence,

00:45:38.810 --> 00:45:43.810
as well as the ability of
human traffickers, terrorists

00:45:43.810 --> 00:45:45.190
and others to organize.

00:45:45.190 --> 00:45:50.190
So we have engaged in, let's
say over the last 15 years,

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:55.140
we've completely changed
our method of discourse

00:45:55.140 --> 00:46:00.140
and it's not exactly surprising
that norms haven't kept up.

00:46:01.370 --> 00:46:05.065
Whether those norms will
ever reach where we are

00:46:05.065 --> 00:46:08.060
in social media, I don't know.

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:11.560
I'd like to think.
circling back to the topic,

00:46:11.560 --> 00:46:14.386
that colleges and universities can be,

00:46:14.386 --> 00:46:17.980
so it both works against
the corrosive effects

00:46:17.980 --> 00:46:21.310
of social media, but it's
hard because our students

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:24.170
are the very ones who are most wired in

00:46:24.170 --> 00:46:28.180
and who have now, you
know, in their short lives,

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:30.450
had lives which have
been completely dominated

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:32.320
by social media.

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:33.260
- Okay. Thank you.

00:46:33.260 --> 00:46:35.970
So we're getting really
a lot of questions here.

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:38.350
So here's an interesting one

00:46:38.350 --> 00:46:41.920
and one that I've faced before too,

00:46:41.920 --> 00:46:43.640
certainly running a program devoted

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:45.427
to the study of antisemitism.

00:46:45.427 --> 00:46:50.427
Do you think at all that
the calling out antisemitism

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:57.530
can result in shutting down
dialogue on college campus

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:00.490
and how to negotiate that.

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:01.550
You know, how-
- Sure.

00:47:01.550 --> 00:47:02.643
- Yeah.
- Yes.

00:47:03.719 --> 00:47:08.570
Look, no one, no side
is right all the time.

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:11.560
And there are certainly been accusations

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:14.040
of antisemitism which are incorrect,

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:17.180
and I'll say there have
certainly been people

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:22.180
who have tried to use the
weapon of antisemitism

00:47:22.290 --> 00:47:23.690
to shut down debate.

00:47:23.690 --> 00:47:27.930
That's happened, and it's a
possibility, and as I noted

00:47:27.930 --> 00:47:29.990
at the beginning of my talk, you know,

00:47:29.990 --> 00:47:32.475
sometimes I'm ambivalent about
talking about antisemitism

00:47:32.475 --> 00:47:36.023
because it immediately sucks
all the oxygen out of the room.

00:47:36.023 --> 00:47:40.330
Because you can't argue,
well, I just disagree with you

00:47:40.330 --> 00:47:44.141
without going to an antisemitic problem.

00:47:44.141 --> 00:47:49.141
How to negotiate that is very difficult.

00:47:49.467 --> 00:47:52.590
Sharansky has a three-part test.

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:54.280
I think that's interesting in terms

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:56.377
of how Israel is treated.

00:47:56.377 --> 00:48:01.260
I opted for something more
ambiguous in my talk, frankly,

00:48:01.260 --> 00:48:02.860
and I give a lot of thought to this

00:48:02.860 --> 00:48:07.100
in preparation for this talk.

00:48:07.100 --> 00:48:12.100
I think it depends on
whether those talking

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:16.490
have exhibited some kind
of reasoned approach

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:18.473
to where they got to their position.

00:48:19.870 --> 00:48:23.650
That's hard, that's easy
to say and hard to discern,

00:48:23.650 --> 00:48:26.170
but I'm gonna stick with it for the moment

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:29.870
because I'm not willing to
immediately shut down debate.

00:48:29.870 --> 00:48:31.383
I think that's bad.

00:48:32.270 --> 00:48:37.270
So I wanna say, I wanna
challenge people to say,

00:48:37.551 --> 00:48:38.773
how did you get here?

00:48:40.453 --> 00:48:44.900
What was the process that you got there?

00:48:44.900 --> 00:48:47.460
And if we can discuss that process,

00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:50.240
which is a long and difficult discourse,

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:53.370
then maybe I still disagree with you,

00:48:53.370 --> 00:48:55.393
but I respect your sentiments.

00:48:56.484 --> 00:48:59.710
And I think that's the kind of discourse

00:48:59.710 --> 00:49:03.623
that colleges and universities
should be especially good at.

00:49:04.550 --> 00:49:07.570
- Okay, so what do you think,

00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:11.590
this is maybe a touchy question,

00:49:11.590 --> 00:49:14.268
but you can answer it how you'd like.

00:49:14.268 --> 00:49:18.690
What do you think President
Salovey should have done

00:49:18.690 --> 00:49:23.690
in response to, let's not
say the YCC statement,

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:28.700
but the ERM and Women's
Studies statements.

00:49:28.700 --> 00:49:33.010
What would your advice
to him have been to do?

00:49:33.010 --> 00:49:34.200
- Right.

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:38.583
You know, I'm hopefully modest

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:42.520
in my recommendations of a campus

00:49:42.520 --> 00:49:44.930
I haven't been on in a long
time and in a situation

00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:46.790
that I don't know well.

00:49:46.790 --> 00:49:50.730
I believe that these issues

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:53.070
and, you know, the statement from,

00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:55.490
the first statement is
on the Yale website.

00:49:55.490 --> 00:49:58.360
The second statement I pulled off the web.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:01.650
I believe that these
issues and these statements

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:04.839
and the issues they raised
rose to the importance

00:50:04.839 --> 00:50:08.180
that a college administrator should say,

00:50:08.180 --> 00:50:12.998
look, this is problematic
from the institution's stance.

00:50:12.998 --> 00:50:17.580
This is just over the line.

00:50:17.580 --> 00:50:19.540
It is not about academic freedom,

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:23.716
which is assigned to individuals,
not programs or centers.

00:50:23.716 --> 00:50:27.900
It is about the institution's
ability to conduct

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:31.430
its core enterprise,
intellectual discourse,

00:50:31.430 --> 00:50:35.433
and I would've recommended
to any president

00:50:35.433 --> 00:50:39.200
who had centers or programs on this,

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:44.200
that they make clear their
opposition and explain,

00:50:45.800 --> 00:50:48.850
because I think this is
an important moment why.

00:50:48.850 --> 00:50:52.115
This is not in this case,
an issue about Israel.

00:50:52.115 --> 00:50:54.420
I don't think it is.

00:50:54.420 --> 00:50:56.730
I don't think the Department of Economics

00:50:56.730 --> 00:50:58.520
should be issuing
statements about how much

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:01.430
deficit spending is appropriate,
even though I'm sure

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:04.800
economists have all
kinds of views about it.

00:51:04.800 --> 00:51:08.470
It's just not what college
programs should be doing.

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:11.720
So I would've advised them to oppose it.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:13.280
- And it's interesting. I think,

00:51:13.280 --> 00:51:18.210
that in general, Political
Science Departments

00:51:18.210 --> 00:51:20.830
are really the least likely to issue

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:23.760
these kinds of statements,
at least it seems like,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.123
or to pass BDS resolutions, it seems.

00:51:27.993 --> 00:51:29.930
You know, I don't know if
you could say something

00:51:29.930 --> 00:51:31.960
about why you think that these

00:51:32.810 --> 00:51:36.568
more identity-focused programs have been

00:51:36.568 --> 00:51:39.713
the ones to do this.

00:51:41.200 --> 00:51:44.917
- You know, I haven't been in the debates

00:51:44.917 --> 00:51:46.640
that had these programs had.

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:50.190
I wasn't part of the processes
that were taken where

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:54.743
these votes, where votes were
taken, if votes were taken.

00:51:55.610 --> 00:52:00.610
I think it goes back to
what you asked before,

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:06.240
which is advocacy and
scholarship, in some areas,

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:11.980
is more directly manifests itself.

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:13.970
Not only in these areas.

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:16.607
I have friends who are
constitutional scholars

00:52:16.607 --> 00:52:19.380
who are in Political Science departments

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:21.700
who have their own advocacy, for sure,

00:52:21.700 --> 00:52:23.503
of their own political positions.

00:52:24.410 --> 00:52:29.290
But the scholars in both programs, take,

00:52:29.290 --> 00:52:34.180
as part of their intellectual
enterprise argument,

00:52:35.840 --> 00:52:39.150
political arguments that their programs

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:40.723
should adopt these platforms.

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:44.500
And so, that's the best that I can say

00:52:44.500 --> 00:52:47.510
without being part of
the debates themselves.

00:52:47.510 --> 00:52:51.110
- Yeah, I mean, you know,
my experience is that,

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:56.110
and I was involved in some of the efforts

00:52:56.580 --> 00:52:59.350
to keep the Modern Language Association

00:52:59.350 --> 00:53:03.340
from adopting a pro-BDS platform,

00:53:03.340 --> 00:53:05.845
and I really thought that
was gonna pass easily

00:53:05.845 --> 00:53:10.320
because there seemed to be a
lot of very strong supporters

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:11.930
and what really emerged was

00:53:11.930 --> 00:53:15.290
that there were a lot of
very, there was a small group,

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:20.290
a relatively small group of
very committed pro-BDS people,

00:53:21.030 --> 00:53:23.790
but that they were the
ones making the most noise.

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:26.935
And that in fact, the
larger membership of the MLA

00:53:26.935 --> 00:53:31.935
was not so anti-Israel, and
I think there's some of that,

00:53:32.876 --> 00:53:37.876
that those voices tend to be
the ones kind of controlling

00:53:39.249 --> 00:53:40.860
what's going on.

00:53:40.860 --> 00:53:41.980
I don't know if you've found that.

00:53:41.980 --> 00:53:46.163
- Well, you know, I think
that's often the case.

00:53:47.400 --> 00:53:50.190
I would say that people
who may (audio cuts out),

00:53:50.190 --> 00:53:52.620
another manifestation of
the problematic nature

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:55.190
of these proclamations is
that you will now be asked

00:53:55.190 --> 00:53:57.800
your political opinion
on every single issue.

00:53:57.800 --> 00:54:00.685
What about this? What about that?

00:54:00.685 --> 00:54:05.140
And some programs, I think,

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:08.860
have found themselves bogged
down in endless debates

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:11.900
about their political positions,
and they should've never

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.110
gone down that route in the first place.

00:54:14.110 --> 00:54:18.660
As I said, if individuals
wanna scholarship and advocacy,

00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:20.190
that's their business.

00:54:20.190 --> 00:54:22.749
It's when you invoke
institutional authority

00:54:22.749 --> 00:54:26.970
that it becomes, I think,
problematic for the university

00:54:26.970 --> 00:54:31.970
and challenging for supporters
and others on campuses.

00:54:32.010 --> 00:54:33.710
- Okay, and what do you, I
don't know if you can say

00:54:33.710 --> 00:54:38.710
anything about the effort
to promote the working,

00:54:38.740 --> 00:54:42.690
the so-called working
definition of antisemitism

00:54:42.690 --> 00:54:45.366
as a kind of campus speech code.

00:54:45.366 --> 00:54:48.060
Do you have any feelings about that?

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:50.560
- I'm opposed to speech codes.

00:54:50.560 --> 00:54:55.270
Before we get, even into the particulars

00:54:55.270 --> 00:54:58.550
of which definition of antisemitism,

00:54:58.550 --> 00:55:01.142
I think colleges and universities,

00:55:01.142 --> 00:55:05.853
when they start regulating
speech, are in for trouble.

00:55:07.577 --> 00:55:10.080
And I think it's
particularly inappropriate

00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:12.730
on a college or university campus.

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:17.147
- What, here's a question
that I've seen echoed

00:55:17.147 --> 00:55:19.810
and several of the people have asked,

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:22.380
and I'll ask it in two parts.

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:26.777
So, first of all, what
would you say to parents

00:55:27.940 --> 00:55:32.940
of students who, many Jewish
students who supported

00:55:35.192 --> 00:55:37.380
that YCC statement?

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:38.620
I think there are a lot of parents

00:55:38.620 --> 00:55:41.120
who are confused about, you know,

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:44.160
how Jewish students now could sign on

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:47.070
to what seems like an
anti-Israel statement.

00:55:47.070 --> 00:55:49.620
So, first of all, how
do you, I'm sure you,

00:55:49.620 --> 00:55:51.950
as the president of
American Jewish University,

00:55:51.950 --> 00:55:53.550
you might have some experience.

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:58.300
And then also, what you would
advise, what you would tell

00:55:58.300 --> 00:56:02.420
a Jewish student on campus
now who feels uncomfortable

00:56:02.420 --> 00:56:06.790
in the current situation,
what advice you would give.

00:56:06.790 --> 00:56:09.190
- Those are two profound questions

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:11.800
and my children, all in their 20s,

00:56:11.800 --> 00:56:14.610
graduated from college not that long ago.

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:18.040
Look, on the first
question, there are a lot

00:56:18.040 --> 00:56:21.963
of intergenerational debates
about Israel right now

00:56:21.963 --> 00:56:26.083
in our society, including among families.

00:56:28.280 --> 00:56:33.280
I would say, talk to your
students, take them seriously.

00:56:33.930 --> 00:56:36.390
Don't believe that
they've been brainwashed

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:39.140
and understand where they're coming from.

00:56:39.140 --> 00:56:41.960
If you disagree with
them, argue with them.

00:56:41.960 --> 00:56:44.746
Not in terms of parental authority,

00:56:44.746 --> 00:56:49.746
but take them seriously and
take their arguments seriously.

00:56:54.574 --> 00:56:58.040
And you may find that you reach

00:56:58.040 --> 00:57:00.764
a meeting of the minds, or you may not.

00:57:00.764 --> 00:57:04.370
You can't always reach
agreement, but I think

00:57:04.370 --> 00:57:07.110
all relationships are improved when people

00:57:07.110 --> 00:57:08.683
take each other seriously.

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:13.490
In terms of Jewish students,
and I'll refer back

00:57:13.490 --> 00:57:15.800
to the story of the beginning,

00:57:15.800 --> 00:57:19.260
I tell the guidance counselor
who advised the person,

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:22.063
the student, not to apply to
Yale that they were wrong.

00:57:22.910 --> 00:57:27.910
I would say that's the ultimate
victory for the antisemites,

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:32.620
that first of all, I don't believe

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.460
that Jewish students shouldn't go to Yale.

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:38.070
I believe they should, but I
believe that Jewish students

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:40.665
like everyone else, should
go wherever they want,

00:57:40.665 --> 00:57:43.250
and should apply to wherever they want,

00:57:43.250 --> 00:57:45.780
and for the students who are afraid,

00:57:45.780 --> 00:57:50.720
sometimes legitimately, I'd
say that this is the moment

00:57:50.720 --> 00:57:54.260
to organize and to be more forthright

00:57:54.260 --> 00:57:57.140
about your political
positions and identity,

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:59.590
to seek allies on the campus community

00:57:59.590 --> 00:58:04.310
and with college administrators,
but that, in the end,

00:58:07.430 --> 00:58:11.873
I believe that antisemitism
will be furthered

00:58:13.730 --> 00:58:17.970
if we try to hide from
it rather confront it.

00:58:17.970 --> 00:58:21.000
Now, I recognize easy for me to say,

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:24.621
I occupy a particular position on a campus

00:58:24.621 --> 00:58:29.621
and students are naturally,
often, conflict averse,

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:32.191
afraid to lose friends and the like,

00:58:32.191 --> 00:58:34.795
but this is the world in which we live.

00:58:34.795 --> 00:58:38.960
And I think if you can develop the tools,

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:43.540
the attitude, the mindset to
not only have a set of beliefs,

00:58:43.540 --> 00:58:45.690
but to feel comfortable with them

00:58:45.690 --> 00:58:47.453
and not to feel threatened,

00:58:48.380 --> 00:58:50.330
that's an important set of life skills.

00:58:51.580 --> 00:58:52.413
- Okay. Terrific.

00:58:52.413 --> 00:58:57.390
Well, this gets us right to
the end of our time today.

00:58:57.390 --> 00:58:59.888
I wanna encourage people
to read your great article

00:58:59.888 --> 00:59:02.050
from over the summer, and I think,

00:59:02.050 --> 00:59:03.740
if I don't have the link in front of me,

00:59:03.740 --> 00:59:06.606
but if you Google Jeffrey
Herbst and Inside Higher Ed,

00:59:06.606 --> 00:59:08.900
it'll come right up.

00:59:08.900 --> 00:59:11.500
So please, you know, you're
not gonna be able to see

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:15.360
all the clapping hands, but
I will do it for everybody.

00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:18.350
And on behalf of our very
large audience today,

00:59:18.350 --> 00:59:21.217
I wanna just say thank you for
taking the time to do this,

00:59:21.217 --> 00:59:23.390
and for your thoughtful presentation.

00:59:23.390 --> 00:59:25.840
And I encourage everyone who is here today

00:59:25.840 --> 00:59:30.840
